A||||2||Phil||Steer me right||||1||
Z||000000||Phil||03-08-2000||12:21 PM||||Hey Gang<BR>What's involved in replacing the bushings in an 88GT steering rack, one side flops up and down about 1/8"? No back and forth movement just up and down.  The kid's car- mother is concerned.||||reg||1
Z||000001||batboy||03-08-2000||12:48 PM||||Rodney Dickman has aluminum rack bushings for the Fiero rack (but I'm sure you knew that). He told me that the passenger side wears out fast because it's a plastic bushing. This one bushing is about the only thing that can be done to fix the rack by the average shade tree mechanic without taking the whole rack off and having it rebuilt or buying another rack. See his website and give him a call for more expert info, he's very helpful:<P> <A HREF="http://www.execpc.com/~rodney1/88rackbushing.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.execpc.com/~rodney1/88rackbushing.htm</A> <P><BR>The bushing can be replaced with the rack on the car, but you have to remove the passenger side inner tie rod in order to get at it. Generally, you'll need to have the car aligned again after farting around with the tie rods.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Ozzy||03-09-2000||03:15 PM||||I just installed Rodney's aluminum replacement in the passenger side of my '88 coupe steering rack.<P>Not too hard to do and it works fine. Good machining and toleraces. I took the tie rod apart at the inner joint and left the rack on the car. Had to improvise to get 90 deg holes drilled for the rivets to hold the bushing in. The price is right too. <P>Ozzy||||reg||1
